**Read:** [Introduction to Historical Geology — Estrada and contributing authors](../sources/Introduction-to-Historical-Geology.lingua#r_1JKSSECEDW0M9QG30TJ1D9KHZD), the full basin, sedimentary-history, and unconformity treatments; [Physical Geology, second edition — Steven Earle](../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX), chapters 6, 9, and regional-history applications; [Groundwater — Freeze and Cherry, preserved authorized edition](../sources/Groundwater-Freeze-Cherry.lingua#r_6P2FK74P9MC2672X94GE1S0NZB), burial/material properties relevant to the chosen example.

A sedimentary basin's preserved fill reflects subsidence/uplift, sediment delivery, erosion, water level, compaction, diagenesis, and tectonic history. Accommodation describes potential space under a reference; it is not identical to deposited thickness or sediment supply. A basin can remain underfilled or lose part of its record.

Distinguish measured thickness, true stratigraphic thickness, decompacted thickness, and inferred burial depth. Compaction and cementation change porosity and volume; erosion removes evidence. A simple thickness/age average does not measure every interval's sedimentation rate and can hide long gaps or short intense events.

Thermal and fluid histories influence maturation, mineral alteration, cementation, permeability, and mechanical behavior. Correlation between a present depth and temperature does not establish the full past maximum temperature or timing. Integrate ages, facies, structures, and appropriate thermal indicators.

**Work:** draw a burial/subsidence history for a published teaching example, keeping observed section/ages separate from decompaction and erosion assumptions. Compare a tectonic and a sediment-supply explanation for a thickness pattern. Identify evidence that constrains an unconformity's duration and the range of material removed.

Read the full basin/environment material and connect it with isostasy, thermal models, and stratigraphic correlation. The practical aim is a defensible historical model useful for groundwater, resources, and infrastructure questions, with the specialized reservoir, geochemistry, and basin-analysis methods left available for further depth.

